It is possible that one could finish the argument here by considering a second moment, and choosing some explicit $f_1$ and $f_2$. To avoid calculating a second moment, we argue as follows. Suppose for contradiction that there were no functions $f_1,\dots,f_d$ that satisfied (\ref{conclusion repeated}). Then, by (\ref{lower bound on solution count of coupled random sets}), if we pick $p$ to be small enough in terms of $\delta$ we have
